 "equations": [
  {
   "name": "Output voltage",
   "expr": "VOUT = (ISHUNT × RSHUNT) × 20",
   "where": {
    "ISHUNT": "load current through the shunt",
    "RSHUNT": "shunt resistance"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 10
  },
  {
   "name": "Inverting node voltage",
   "expr": "V(inverting) = VCM − (ISHUNT × RSHUNT)",
   "where": {
    "VCM": "common-mode voltage at the inputs"
   },
   "note": "Almost no current flows in the inverting-side input resistor R.",
   "page": 10
  },
  {
   "name": "Input-stage current",
   "expr": "IIN = (ISHUNT × RSHUNT) / R1",
   "where": {
    "IIN": "current through the internal input resistor R1, turned back into a voltage across ROUT",
    "R1": "internal input resistor on the noninverting side"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 10
  },
  {
   "name": "Supply current at low common-mode voltage",
   "expr": "IS = −0.275 (VCM) + 2.5",
   "where": {
    "IS": "supply current, mA",
    "VCM": "input common-mode voltage, V, below 5 V"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 3
  }
 ],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "How it works",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "The load current makes a small voltage across the external shunt, which reaches amplifier A1 through two input resistors, R on the inverting side and R1 on the noninverting side. Negligible current flows in R, so the inverting node sits at VCM − ISHUNT × RSHUNT; A1 drives the noninverting node to that same potential, so a current of ISHUNT × RSHUNT / R1 flows through R1 and transistor Q1 into ROUT, giving a ground-referenced voltage. A buffer with a gain of 20 V/V follows; its gain-setting resistors are trimmed to match within 0.01 %. The part contains proprietary offset circuitry."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Common-mode range and supply",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The inputs work from −2 V to +65 V common mode and withstand −3 V to +68 V continuously, independent of the 5 V supply. Rejection is 120 dB typ at dc above 5 V common mode and 90 dB typ below it. Below 5 V common mode the input impedance falls to 3.5 kΩ and the supply current rises (see the equation)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output linearity at small inputs",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "The input stage keeps the transfer linear even with a very small differential input and a common-mode voltage below the 5 V supply. The output is correct for differential inputs of 2 mV and above at any common-mode voltage, since the output amplifier swings down to 33 mV typ; the specified minimum output of 100 mV leaves a guard band. Linear range: 0 mV to 250 mV differential."
  },
  {
   "topic": "High-side shunt, low-side switch",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "With the shunt between a solenoid and a ground-side PWM switch, current keeps flowing through the shunt after the switch opens, so recirculating current is measured as well, and a short to ground shows up. The common-mode voltage swings from near ground (switch on) to a diode drop above the battery (switch off, clamp conducting)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Battery-referenced shunt",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "With the shunt tied to the battery the inputs see the full battery voltage; recirculating current is still measured and a short to ground still shows up; and the output is linear and ground-referenced. An AD8214 on the same shunt can add an overcurrent signal within 100 ns."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Ground-referenced shunt",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "In low-side sensing the part rejects ground noise with CMRR typically above 90 dB, and the output stays linear across the differential input range."
  }
 ],
